  "knowledgePattern": "Expand a radical quotient into powers before differentiating",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "calculus"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The domain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Expand the numerator and divide each term by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>The power rule gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Answer B.</p>",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Rewrite the quotient first",
      "body": "<p>Expand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> and write the denominator as a single power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "When dividing powers of the same positive base, subtract exponents.",
      "pitfall": "Divide every numerator term by the denominator.",
      "pause": "Turn the whole quotient into a sum of powers.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l05",
          "reminder": "When dividing powers of the same positive base, subtract exponents."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Differentiate before substituting",
      "body": "<p>Apply the power rule to each term, then put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> into the derivative.</p>",
      "recap": "For a power term, multiply by its exponent and reduce that exponent by one.",
      "pitfall": "Substituting before differentiating would replace the curve by one constant value.",
      "pause": "Calculate the derivative and add its values exactly.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "For a power term, multiply by its exponent and reduce that exponent by one."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
